---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DIG, SUPPORT, CACHE_TTLS, SERVICE } from "../config/brand";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy Policy — WOIII.me"
  description="What WOIII.me's Chrome extension, ChatGPT and Claude plugins, and the earlier DIG for Discogs service process, why, where, and for how long."
  bodyClass="privacy-page"
  headerVariant="overlay"
>
  <section class="section privacy-section" data-header-tone="dark">
    <div class="wrap policy">
      <h1>Privacy <span class="accent">Policy</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The short version: {SERVICE.name} reads the Discogs data it needs to
        answer what you ask. It is read-only, shows no prices or cover images,
        never receives your conversations, does not sell personal data, and
        does not use advertising or behavioural tracking.
      </p>
      <p class="updated">
        Last updated: 21 October 2026 · Applies to {SERVICE.name}: the Chrome
        side panel extension, the {SERVICE.name} plugin and connector for
        ChatGPT and Claude, and the server they use; to {SERVICE.formerName},
        the earlier version; and to this website.
      </p>

      <h2>Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        {SERVICE.name} is an independent project. It operates the services
        above and is responsible for the personal data described here. It is
        not affiliated with, sponsored or endorsed by Discogs. Contact:{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT.email}`}>{SUPPORT.email}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the service runs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>{SERVICE.name} server</strong> at <code>{SERVICE.serverHost}</code>.
          The Chrome extension from the Chrome Web Store and the ChatGPT and
          Claude plugins connect here. It will move to <code>{SERVICE.plannedHost}</code>;
          this policy covers both addresses.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>{SERVICE.formerName}</strong>, the earlier version, at{" "}
          <code>{SERVICE.legacyHost}</code>. Where it differs, see{" "}
          <a href="#earlier-version">The earlier version</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Data we process</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Discogs sign-in.</strong> When you connect, Discogs asks you
          to authorise {SERVICE.name}. We then receive an OAuth access token and
          secret for your Discogs account, your Discogs username and your
          numeric Discogs user ID. We never see your Discogs password.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your requests.</strong> The arguments the extension or your
          AI assistant sends for each task: a Discogs release or master link or
          ID, an album or artist to search for, pressings to compare, or a
          settings change. We do not receive your conversation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Discogs data you ask for.</strong> Catalogue data (releases,
          masters and their versions, credits, matrix and runout details,
          labels, formats, community ratings and have/want counts) and, when you
          ask for it, your own collection and wantlist. We do not request,
          store or show marketplace prices, listings or images.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your settings.</strong> Default lens, how many pressings are
          checked per album, whether CDs and SACDs may be recommended, and
          whether explanatory notes show.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Technical data.</strong> Our hosting providers see your IP
          address and ordinary request details in order to deliver the service.
          We do not write IP addresses into our logs or statistics.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why we use it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          To provide what you ask for: sign-in, pressing verdicts, surveys and
          comparisons, and collection and wantlist features. This processing is
          necessary to provide the service you request.
        </li>
        <li>
          To stay within Discogs' rate limits, keep the service reliable and
          prevent abuse. This is our legitimate interest in running a stable,
          compliant service.
        </li>
        <li>
          To see, in aggregate and without any user identifier, which features
          and albums are used, so we can improve the scoring. This is also our
          legitimate interest.
        </li>
        <li>To answer support, privacy and security requests.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We do not use your data for advertising, sell it, use it to assess
        creditworthiness, or use it to train AI models.
      </p>

      <h2>What we store, and for how long</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your sign-in grant</strong> is stored on our server
          (Cloudflare Workers KV). The Discogs token, secret and username are
          encrypted inside it; the grant also records your numeric Discogs user
          ID. It is kept while your connection exists. Revoking{" "}
          {SERVICE.name} on Discogs makes the token unusable at once, and we
          delete the grant on request.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Discogs catalogue responses</strong> are cached for at most{" "}
          {CACHE_TTLS.catalogue} after they were fetched, so nothing we show is
          more than six hours old.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your collection and wantlist pages, and the account behind a
          personal access token,</strong> are cached for at most{" "}
          {CACHE_TTLS.collectionsAndWantlists}. Collection entries are keyed by
          your Discogs username; a personal access token is stored only as a
          one-way hash.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your settings</strong> are stored under a one-way hash of your
          Discogs user ID and kept for {CACHE_TTLS.settings} after the last
          change. Ask the assistant to reset your {SERVICE.name} settings to
          delete them at once.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sign-in state</strong> (the step between our server and
          Discogs) expires after {CACHE_TTLS.signInState}.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Usage statistics</strong> (Cloudflare Workers Analytics
          Engine): one record per action and per Discogs request, holding the
          action name, outcome, timings, counts, rate-limit readings and the
          Cloudflare data-centre code. For album surveys it also holds the
          album's public Discogs master ID. No record holds a username, user ID,
          token, IP address, search text or URL. Cloudflare keeps them for{" "}
          {CACHE_TTLS.analytics}.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Error and operations logs</strong> (Cloudflare Workers Logs)
          use a closed set of fields with no IDs, usernames, tokens, IP
          addresses, search text or URLs, and are kept for up to{" "}
          {CACHE_TTLS.logs}.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Support correspondence</strong> is kept only as long as needed
          to resolve the request, keep security records or meet legal
          obligations.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who receives or processes data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Discogs</strong> receives the API requests needed to answer
          you; requests for your own collection or wantlist are signed with your
          token. Discogs handles them under its own terms and privacy policy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts our server, its storage, the usage
          statistics and the logs, as our infrastructure provider.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fly.io</strong> runs a small relay in {SERVICE.relayRegion}
          that sends our Discogs requests from one fixed address, so we stay
          within Discogs' rate limit. It passes requests through and logs only
          each request's status code, timing and remaining rate-limit count.
          Discogs sees the relay's address, not yours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your AI provider.</strong> When you use {SERVICE.name} in
          ChatGPT (OpenAI) or Claude (Anthropic), that provider processes your
          conversation, the tool request and the result we return, under its own
          privacy policy. Results can remain in your chat history according to
          your settings there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google</strong> distributes the extension through the Chrome
          Web Store and gives us only aggregate install statistics.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vercel</strong> hosts this website.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Services you choose for support.</strong> Email you send us,
          or a public GitHub issue you open, is processed by that email or
          GitHub service.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These providers operate in the European Union and the United States;
        transfers outside the EU rely on their standard contractual clauses. We
        disclose data to others only when the law requires it.
      </p>

      <h2>Chrome extension</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The side panel reads the address of the discogs.com tab you are
          viewing to know which release or master page is open. On a
          marketplace listing page it reads that page's release link. It has no
          access to other sites, unless you enter your own server address in
          its settings; Chrome then asks your permission for that one address.
        </li>
        <li>
          It sends release and master IDs and your requests only to the{" "}
          {SERVICE.name} server.
        </li>
        <li>
          It stores on your device: the sign-in session token issued by our
          server (never your Discogs password or Discogs token), your Discogs
          username (shown only on its settings page), your settings and accent
          colour, and your last 10 checked pressings (title, artist and score),
          which it shows only while that data is under six hours old. Answers
          are also kept in memory for up to {CACHE_TTLS.extensionMemory}.
        </li>
        <li>
          Signing out removes the session, username, recent list and cached
          answers from your device. Uninstalling removes everything.
        </li>
        <li>
          It does not collect browsing history, run remote code, show ads or
          contain analytics.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        {SERVICE.name}'s use of information received through the extension
        adheres to the{" "}
        <a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/webstore/program-policies/user-data-faq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chrome Web Store User Data Policy</a>,
        including the Limited Use requirements. That information is used only
        to provide the extension's single purpose. It is not sold, not used or
        transferred for advertising, creditworthiness or lending, and not read
        by people except with your permission, for security, or as the law
        requires.
      </p>

      <h2>ChatGPT and Claude</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Each tool call carries only the arguments your assistant chose for
          that request. We do not receive or store conversation transcripts.
        </li>
        <li>
          ChatGPT may attach hints such as your locale, user agent or an
          anonymised user ID to a call. We neither read nor store them.
        </li>
        <li>
          Results contain Discogs-derived data with "Data provided by Discogs"
          links. The cards shown in the chat load code only from our server and
          contain no third-party content or trackers.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="earlier-version">The earlier version ({SERVICE.formerName})</h2>
      <p>
        {SERVICE.formerName} is being replaced by {SERVICE.name}. While it runs,
        it differs in these ways: it may return marketplace price fields where
        Discogs provides them; it may cache the account behind a personal access
        token for up to 24 hours; its recently-analysed list can show data up
        to about 12 hours old; and it may send public Discogs release-notes text
        (never account data) to TypeSafe AI, which classifies pressing claims in
        that text.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Revoke {SERVICE.name}'s access at any time in your Discogs settings,
          under <em>Applications</em>. Its Discogs token stops working at once.
        </li>
        <li>Sign out of the extension, or uninstall it, to clear it from your device.</li>
        <li>Disconnect the plugin or connector in ChatGPT or Claude to stop it from calling {SERVICE.name}.</li>
        <li>Ask your assistant to reset your {SERVICE.name} settings.</li>
        <li>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT.email}`}>{SUPPORT.email}</a> with
          "Privacy request" in the subject to ask for access, correction,
          deletion (including your sign-in grant and settings), restriction,
          objection or a copy of your data. We answer within one month and may
          need your Discogs username to find the data.
        </li>
        <li>
          You can also complain to the data protection authority where you
          live.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Security</h2>
      <p>
        Traffic is encrypted in transit. Discogs credentials are encrypted
        inside the sign-in grant, personal access tokens are stored only as
        hashes, and the service asks Discogs only for read access. Report a
        suspected security problem privately; see <a href="/support">Support</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        {SERVICE.name} is not intended for children under 16, and we do not
        knowingly process their data.
      </p>

      <h2>Data we do not request</h2>
      <p>
        Do not send payment-card information, health information, government
        identifiers, passwords, API keys, OAuth tokens, MFA codes or other
        secrets through {SERVICE.name} or its support channels.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We update this policy when {SERVICE.name}'s features, providers or
        legal obligations change. The date above marks the current version;
        material changes are also noted in the release notes.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about privacy: <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT.email}`}>{SUPPORT.email}</a>{" "}
        or{" "}<a href={DIG.issuesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Issues</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy-section {
    --accent-hue: #c8ff9f;
    background: #000;
    padding-block: 170px 104px;
  }
  .privacy-section > .policy {
    --text-width: 62rem;
    width: min(100%, 1440px);
    max-width: 1440px;
    padding-inline: clamp(24px, 3.35vw, 48px);
  }
  .policy h2 {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    margin-top: calc(var(--space) * 7);
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--space) * 2.5);
    padding-top: calc(var(--space) * 4);
    max-width: var(--text-width);
  }
  .policy ul {
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    display: grid;
    gap: calc(var(--space) * 1.75);
    max-width: var(--text-width);
  }
  .policy li::marker {
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .policy p {
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--space) * 2);
  }
  .updated {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--ink-tertiary);
    margin-top: calc(var(--space) * 3);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .privacy-section {
      padding-block: 125px 64px;
    }
  }
</style>
